    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This dissertation describes a theoretical and experimental study of the streaming potentials generated during the flow of foam through capillary tubes and porous media. Important parameters affecting the magnitude of the streaming potential have been investigated and a physical model adequately describing the experimental results is presented. Foams were produced from aqueous solutions of various surface active agents in a special foam generator of a novel design. The porous media studied were: (l) capillary tubes of several diameters, (2) a capillary tube packed with glass beads only slightly smaller in diameter than the capillary, and (3) a capillary tube filled with crushed pyrex glass. The quantities measured were streaming potential, streaming current, flow rate and foam quality (ratio of gas volume to total volume of foam).A linear relationship between streaming potential and pressure differential was observed. It was found that the streaming potential developed during the flow is dependent upon the quality of foam, the electrochemical nature of the surface active agent in the foam and the radius of the flow channel. A streaming potential as high as 75 volts was observed when very dry non-ionic foam flowed through a capillary. The value decreased, however, when an ionic foam was used. Electrical tortuosity for foam is closely related to its quality; the value increases gradually from 1.5 to 2.7 for the quality range from 0.50 to 0.80. These values of electrical tortuosity are similar to those for the fluid tortuosity for unconsolidated porous media in the porosity range from 0.50 to 0.20. However, the electrical tortuosity for drier foams, which correspond to porous media of 0.20-0.05 porosity, are greater and may reach values as high as 8.0. In its flow through capillary tubes, foam behaves like a Bingham plastic. Its flow behavior in other porous media is difficult to describe because of the experimental difficulties involved in independent measurements of both foam viscosity and effective permeability. Foam quality is an important parameter in foam flow; it was varied from values of 0.50 for very wet foams to values of 0.96 for dry foams.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
